      "request_id": "bench-qwen36-27b-autoround-int4-b70-realistic-v1-06-architecture-tradeoff",
      "request_started_epoch_s": 1788245663.013008,
      "response_id_first": "cmpl-bench-qwen36-27b-autoround-int4-b70-realistic-v1-06-architecture-tradeoff",
      "response_id_last": "cmpl-bench-qwen36-27b-autoround-int4-b70-realistic-v1-06-architecture-tradeoff",
      "response_x_request_id": null,
      "sha256": "0f96c67facba61f4881ca6f6e9d77e4bf0915b602d7559f2108124d0a1a158f7",
      "stream_token_id_count": 512,
      "text": "\n\nBelow is a structured comparison of the two most common ways to serve a single large model on a 4\u2011GPU node:\n\n- Option A: Tensor parallel (TP) across 4 GPUs (one logical replica)\n- Option B: 4 independent 1\u2011GPU replicas behind a router (data parallel / model parallel by replication)\n\nI\u2019ll assume a typical local inference service (e.g., vLLM, TGI, SGLang, or a custom FastAPI + torch backend) and a model that either fits on one GPU (B) or does not (A).\n\n1) Latency\n\n- TP (4 GPUs)\n  - Time to first token (TTFT): Lower for large models because the forward pass is split across GPUs; each layer\u2019s compute is parallelized.\n  - Inter-token latency (ITL): Can be slightly higher per token due to all-reduce/all-gather communication after each layer. On NVLink this is small; on PCIe it can dominate.\n  - Overall: Best for low-latency, single-request, large-model serving.\n- 4\u00d71\u2011GPU replicas\n  - TTFT: Higher if the model is large and must be computed on a single GPU (more FLOPs per GPU, no layer parallelism).\n  - ITL: Lower per token if the model fits comfortably on one GPU and batch size is small (no cross-GPU sync).\n  - Overall: Good for small/medium models where per-GPU compute is sufficient; latency is dominated by single-GPU compute.\n\n2) Throughput\n\n- TP (4 GPUs)\n  - Throughput per request: High for large models (faster generation).\n  - Aggregate throughput: Limited by the single logical replica; you can batch requests, but all share the same 4-GPU pipeline. Communication overhead grows with batch size.\n  - Best when: You need high per-request speed and can tolerate moderate concurrency.\n- 4\u00d71\u2011GPU replicas\n  - Throughput per request: Lower for large models (single GPU compute).\n  - Aggregate throughput: Higher for small/medium models because you can serve 4\u00d7 the concurrent requests (one per GPU) with no cross-GPU sync.\n  - Best when: You have many small requests and the model fits on one GPU.\n\n3) PCIe Traffic\n\n- TP (4 GPUs)\n  - Heavy: All-reduce/all-gather after each layer",
      "text_preview": "\n\nBelow is a structured comparison of the two most common ways to serve a single large model on a 4\u2011GPU node:\n\n- Option A: Tensor parallel (TP) across 4 GPUs (one logical replica)\n- Option B: 4 independent 1\u2011GPU replicas behind a router (data parallel / model parallel by replication)\n\nI\u2019ll assume a typical local infere",
